(D6 — Discrepancy 6 repro: `Return a file, <handle>.` is accepted, and the
 returned handle is usable — but only if it is received with `Set`. The
 `a file called X is <call>.` declaration form does not track the type,
 and the first property read off X is rejected with the caret on the
 DECLARATION. LANGUAGE.md:753-755 lists `file` among the eleven legal
 return types and :748-750 says "a function call's declared return type is
 tracked through assignment".
 The strongest pro-compiler reading is that this is not about functions
 at all: `a file called alias is src.` — aliasing one top-level file
 variable to another, no call in sight — is rejected identically, so the
 `a file called X is <expr>` form simply does not exist and the call is
 an innocent bystander. The `Set` form below proves the value itself is
 fine.
